{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Completion verifier reads a stale ticket snapshot — CONFIRMED DEFECT (plus a second, independent bare-FAIL defect)\n\n## Disposition (investigation complete)\n\nHypothesis 1 (**staleness defect**) CONFIRMED; hypothesis 3 (**a non-itemized FAIL is\nindistinguishable from \"no verdict obtainable\"**) CONFIRMED as a SECOND, independent defect.\nHypothesis 2 (deliberate anti-gaming snapshot) REJECTED: the pin is deliberate, but its documented\npurpose is reproducibility/signability and its own docstring states the goal is to pin \"the shared\nstore, **not a stale local copy**\" (`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:546-548`) — staleness is\ncontrary to intent, not the intent.\n\n### Root cause 1 — the close path pins the ticket store with no fetch\n\n`close_precheck` calls the verifier with `fetch=False`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py:505-512`), justified in its own comment (`:502-504`) only\nfor the CODE ref (`HEAD`, always local). `resolve_gate_handle` threads the same flag into the\nTICKET store pin (`src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:115`), and in `materialize_tickets`\n(`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:568-576`) `fetch=False` skips `origin/tickets` and pins the\nmain repo's local `refs/heads/tickets` — a mirror that only advances on fetch. `.tickets-tracker/`\nis a SEPARATE repository, so that mirror is arbitrarily stale (measured here: 6757 commits behind).\nReproduced deterministically, no LLM call — `materialize_tickets(fetch=False)` then\n`show_ticket(cb91)` yields `{status: open, comments: 0}`, while `fetch=True` yields\n`{status: in_progress, comments: 2}`. That `status: open` + `comments: []` is verbatim what\nincident 1's stored `COMPLETION_VERDICT` cited as its authoritative evidence.\n\n### Root cause 1b — the two halves of the gate read different stores\n\nThe fenced context given to the model comes from the LIVE store (`gate_ops.py:174,190`); the\nmodel's own `show_ticket` reads the PINNED snapshot (`runner.py:335-337`). The prompt labels that\ncontext `<untrusted_ticket_context>` and names `show_ticket` authoritative\n(`completion_verifier.md:26-28`), so the model correctly preferred the stale snapshot.\n\n### Root cause 2 — a FAIL with no findings is fabricated, not reported\n\n`reconcile_verdict` (`completion.py:454-467`) coerces any non-`PASS` verdict to `FAIL`, then, when\n`findings` is empty, SYNTHESIZES a placeholder criterion `(unspecified)` — exactly what incident\n3's stored verdict shows. Unlike plan-review (unsigned `INDETERMINATE`, exit 2), completion\nverification cannot say \"I could not obtain a verdict\".\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n1. **Pin the ticket store at the LIVE store's own HEAD.** `materialize_tickets`\n   (`repo_snapshot.py:534`) resolves the sha from the live tracker repo (`.tickets-tracker`) HEAD\n   when present — the state every other rebar read sees — so the pin is current-at-close by\n   construction and the gate's two halves stop disagreeing. Still immutable-by-sha and signable.\n   `fetch` keeps its meaning (\"may I touch the network?\"), applied uniformly to every caller:\n   - `fetch=True` (`review-plan` via `operations.py:203`, standalone `verify-completion`, MCP):\n     force an UN-throttled reconverge of the tracker with `origin/tickets` (the `_store.sync`\n     implementation `reads.ensure_fresh` uses, called so the <=1/min marker is bypassed).\n     `reconverge` returns `None` on success AND on every failure, so it yields no usable signal;\n     `materialize_tickets` therefore confirms the outcome itself with `git -C <tracker> merge-base\n     --is-ancestor <remote>/tickets HEAD`. Exit 0 (at-or-ahead) => pin tracker HEAD. Non-zero, or\n     the fetch / `rev-parse <remote>/tickets` failing => fall back to today's exact behaviour,\n     `resolve_ref(f\"{remote}/tickets\", fetch=True)`. The \"no content visible today is lost\"\n     invariant thus holds unconditionally, not only when a throttle window is open.\n   - `fetch=False` (close path): pin tracker HEAD as-is — current-at-close, never the stale mirror.\n   - Tracker absent (CI, checkout-less): unchanged `origin/tickets` -> local-branch chain.\n2. **Give completion verification a \"no verdict obtainable\" channel.** Indeterminacy rides on a\n   NEW framework-set boolean `verdict_obtainable`, NOT a third verdict token: the `{PASS, FAIL}`\n   vocabulary, its normalizing validator, and fail-closed blocking stay intact, and no token can\n   be coerced away. Touch points, all of them:\n   - `reconcile_verdict` (`completion.py:442`) — the rule keys on FINDINGS, since `gate_ops`\n     populates `result[\"criteria\"]` before delegating here, so a genuine fault can arrive with a\n     criteria manifest. For a non-PASS verdict with zero `findings`: if `criteria` holds any\n     `met: false` entries, synthesize real findings FROM them (named criteria, NOT a fault); only\n     when no unmet criterion is identifiable from either source, set `verdict_obtainable=false`\n     and emit a finding saying no usable verdict was produced, replacing the fabricated\n     `(unspecified)`. `verdict` stays `FAIL`, so every existing consumer still blocks.\n   - `gate_ops.completion_reconcile` (`workflow/gate_ops.py:297`), the workflow close-gate path\n     that also stamps `certifiable`: inherits the marker by delegating to `reconcile_verdict`;\n     asserted by a test. Such a verdict is never certifiable and never signs, as any FAIL today.\n   - `completion_sidecar` (`completion_sidecar.py:323`): the marker rides onto the durable record\n     so a fault is queryable afterwards rather than looking like an unmet criterion.\n   - `close_precheck.py:543`: check `verdict_obtainable` BEFORE the `!= PASS` branch and route it\n     to a distinct message naming it a verifier fault, with retryable exit 11 instead of 1.\n   - `_cli/_llm_commands.py:383-389` — the standalone `verify-completion` exit mapping, which\n     today flattens every non-PASS verdict to exit 1: route `verdict_obtainable=false` to the same\n     retryable exit 11 `_llm_error_exit_code` uses, so a fault is retryable from either entry\n     point.\n   - `schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json`: document the optional boolean.\n3. **Fix the misleading wording at its real sites.** The \"does not exist\" phrasing is\n   model-generated, so nothing is deleted from the prompt; instead (a)\n   `reviewers/completion_verifier.md` gains an instruction that an absence observed via\n   `show_ticket` must be reported as \"not visible in the ticket snapshot I read\", never as a claim\n   the record does not exist, and (b) `COMPLETION_REMEDIATION_GUIDANCE` (`completion.py:54`) is\n   reworded to match.\n4. **Document the ticket-store pin** in `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md` (today it documents only the\n   code snapshot) and cross-reference it from `docs/plan-review-gate.md`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] [operator-attested] The investigation is settled and durably recorded here: the\n      `show_ticket` read path with its exact pin point, and the verdict (staleness defect, NOT a\n      deliberate review-time snapshot). Evidence: the \"Disposition\" and \"Root cause\" sections of\n      this description, each carrying file:line citations. Already satisfied by the text above;\n      the remaining ACs are the code end-states.\n- [ ] The pinned ticket snapshot reflects the live store at close time, with a regression test\n      that fails before the fix.\n- [ ] The `fetch=True` consumers are covered: a regression test proves a `fetch=True` snapshot\n      still contains everything `origin/tickets` holds (including via the confirm-or-fall-back\n      path). Tracker-repo-absent environments keep the existing resolution.\n- [ ] A non-PASS verdict from which no unmet criterion is identifiable sets\n      `verdict_obtainable=false` and is reported as a verifier fault with a distinct message and\n      retryable exit, not as a fabricated unmet criterion — with a regression test covering\n      `reconcile_verdict` and the workflow path.\n- [ ] The unmet-criterion diagnostic distinguishes \"not present\" from \"not visible in the\n      snapshot read\", and the ticket-store pin is documented in `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md`.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786588788818982001,"uuid":"920fc486-affc-450d-80be-43f68e6e4925"}